Kodstigen has had a C# path for a while. It teaches the language: types, classes, interfaces, generics. What it never covered is the platform underneath, and that is where most of the actual work happens once you leave the tutorial. So there is now a separate **.NET path**, three courses and 21 lessons, from `dotnet new console` to profiling a service that is allocating too much.

## Why .NET and C# are two different paths

C# is a language. .NET is a runtime that JIT-compiles IL, a garbage collector with generational heuristics you can measure, a base class library, a dependency injection container, a web framework, an ORM, and a set of diagnostics tools.

You can know C# syntax perfectly and still not know why your API allocates 400 MB a minute, why injecting a `DbContext` into a singleton breaks on the second request, or why your retry policy turned one user request into 27 calls against a dependency that was already down. Those are platform questions, and they get their own path.

## [.NET Foundations](/courses/dotnet-foundations)

Seven lessons: what the runtime, SDK, and CLI actually are; types and control flow; classes, records, and object initializers; inheritance, interfaces, and pattern matching; collections and generics with a first taste of LINQ; then exceptions, file IO, and NuGet.

It ends with a console expense tracker that has to survive a restart, which quietly forces you to deal with JSON, a corrupt file, and validation that does not crash the app.

## [.NET Intermediate: LINQ, Async & ASP.NET Core](/courses/dotnet-intermediate-linq-async-asp-net-core)

The middle course is where .NET starts to feel like .NET. Grouping and joins in LINQ, custom iterators with `yield return`, `async`/`await` and the task model, cancellation tokens and parallelism, then ASP.NET Core: the middleware pipeline, minimal APIs, dependency injection lifetimes, configuration, and EF Core. The project is a real task API with paging, filtering, and migrations.

The idea that ties the first half together is **deferred execution**: a LINQ query is a recipe, not a result. Nothing runs until something asks for a value. That is not C#-specific, and JavaScript generators show the same mechanic, so you can run it right here:

```javascript
// A lazy pipeline, the way LINQ builds one: nothing runs until something asks.
function* numbers() {
  for (let i = 1; ; i++) {
    console.log('produced', i);
    yield i;
  }
}

function* where(source, predicate) {
  for (const item of source) if (predicate(item)) yield item;
}

function* take(source, count) {
  if (count <= 0) return;
  for (const item of source) {
    yield item;
    if (--count === 0) return;
  }
}

const query = take(where(numbers(), (n) => n % 3 === 0), 2);
console.log('query built, nothing produced yet');
console.log('result:', [...query]);
```

Run it. `numbers()` is an infinite sequence, and the program still terminates: it produces exactly six values, because `take` stops asking after the second match. Swap the `2` for a `3` and count the extra lines. That is the whole reason `orders.Where(...).Take(20)` does not load your database into memory first, and the reason enumerating the same query twice does the work twice.

## [.NET Advanced: Performance, Memory & Architecture](/courses/dotnet-advanced-performance-memory-architecture)

The last course is the production one. The allocation model and what generational GC actually costs (survivors, not allocations). `Span<T>` and `Memory<T>` for parsing without allocating, and why a `Span` cannot cross an `await`. Benchmarking with BenchmarkDotNet and profiling a live process with `dotnet-counters` and `dotnet-trace`. Then layered architecture and CQRS, microservice resilience with timeouts, retries, circuit breakers and the outbox pattern, and a security lesson covering JWT validation, authorization policies, data protection keys, and secrets.

The final project starts from a naive CSV parser and a benchmark of it, and asks you to beat your own baseline by 2x in time and 90% in allocations, then prove it again in a running process rather than only in the microbenchmark. The two numbers disagreeing is itself part of the lesson.

## Opinions, on purpose

The advanced lessons take positions where the guidance is settled. Do not call `GC.Collect()`. Retry only idempotent operations, and never `POST /payments` without an idempotency key. A liveness probe must not check your database, or one slow query restarts the whole fleet. `throw ex;` destroys the stack trace and `throw;` does not.

You are free to disagree with any of it. But an "it depends" written into a lesson helps nobody at 3am, and each of these has a reason attached.

Every code block on the site is editable, so the fastest way in is to open [.NET Foundations](/courses/dotnet-foundations) and start changing things until something breaks.
